{"name":"Aged Olive","hex":"#7e7666","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"aged","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Old.","Having the age of."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Old people, collectively."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/aged"},{"term":"olive","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tree of species Olea europaea cultivated since ancient times in the Mediterranean for its fruit and the oil obtained from it.","The small oval fruit of this tree, eaten ripe (usually black) or unripe (usually green)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish green color, that of an unripe olive."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/olive"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ageing","blocks":[{"text":"Ageing is the process of becoming older. The term refers mainly to humans, many other animals, and fungi; whereas for example, bacteria, perennial plants and some simple animals are potentially biologically immortal. In a broader sense, ageing can refer to single cells within an organism which have ceased dividing, or to the population of a species."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ageing"},{"title":"Olive","blocks":[{"text":"The cultivated olive is a subspecies of subtropical evergreen tree in the family Oleaceae. Modern cultivars are traced primarily to the Near East, Aegean Sea, and Strait of Gibraltar. Olea europaea is the type species for its genus, Olea, which lends its name to the Oleaceae plant family. The olive fruit is classed botanically as a drupe, similar in structure and function to the cherry or peach. The term oil was originally synonymous with olive oil."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Olivesfromjordan.jpg/330px-Olivesfromjordan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive"},{"title":"Olive (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Olive, also called olive green, is a dark shade of yellowish green, like that of unripe or green olives."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg/330px-Olives_stuffed_with_almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Olive_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg/330px-Views_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AViews_of_a_Foetus_in_the_Womb_detail.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/500px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/1280px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Hydras_%288%29.JPG/500px-Hydras_%288%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Immortal Hydra, a relative of the jellyfish","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHydras_(8).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Senescence.JPG/500px-Senescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Senescence.JPG/500px-Senescence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Enlarged ears and noses of old humans are sometimes blamed on continual cartilage growth, but the cause is more probably 